  "text": "umably, we can start by recognizing that we have a system of equations:\n\n1. $x^2 + y^2 = 205$\n2. $x^3 + y^3 = 205$\n\nWe can utilize the identity for the sum of cubes:\n\n$x^3 + y^3 = (x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)$\n\nGiven that $x^3 + y^3 = 205$, we can substitute $x^2 + y^2 = 205$ into the sum of cubes identity:\n\n$x^3 + y^3 = (x + y)(205 - xy)$\n\nSince we know that $x^3 + y^3 = 205$, we can set up the following equation:\n\n$205 = (x + y)(205 - xy)$\n\nNow, we can solve for $xy$ by rearranging the equation:\n\n$205 = 205(x + y) - xy(x + y)$\n\nDivide both sides by $(x + y)$:\n\n$1 = 205 - xy$\n\nSolve for $xy$:\n\n$xy = 205 - 1$\n\n$xy = 204$\n\nTherefore, $xy = 204$.",
